\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) training systems are increasingly deployed as production AI software, yet their runtime failure recovery remains difficult to test, repair, and validate. A single GPU failure can invalidate only part of the training state, but existing recovery mechanisms typically rely on coarse-grained checkpoint restart, forcing the entire training job to reload stale global state and replay lost progress. More importantly, current training software provides limited support for reasoning about whether a partial recovery has preserved model quality, routing stability, and optimizer consistency.
This paper presents MoEGuard, a runtime reliability framework for engineering self-healing distributed MoE training. MoEGuard provides two core capabilities: safe-point repair for preventing invalid optimizer commits, and expert-weighted-staleness guarded recovery for selecting between checkpoint restart and hybrid state restoration. Instead of treating recovery risk as a global property of the training job, MoEGuard tracks an expert-weighted staleness density $\Phi'(t)$ over a sliding exposure window. This design jointly accounts for how many iterations of staleness each hybrid recovery injects and how large a fraction of the expert population has been exposed, enabling the system to use faster hybrid repair when safe and fall back to checkpoint restart when accumulated risk becomes high.
MoEGuard repairs failed ranks using MoE-aware recovery semantics: dense and router states are synchronized from healthy data-parallel neighbors, while affected expert states are restored from distributed checkpoints. When concurrent failures eliminate every healthy data-parallel peer at the failed rank's pipeline/tensor-parallel coordinate (so that no surviving peer holds a current-step replica of the dense/router state), MoEGuard transparently falls back to checkpoint restart, since hybrid recovery is by construction predicated on the existence of at least one such peer. A two-phase recovery protocol restores weights first and attaches optimizer state later to reduce time to resumed training. We implement MoEGuard in Megatron-LM and evaluate it with injected fault traces on H20 GPU clusters. Across single-failure, repeated-failure, concentrated-failure, and distributed-failure scenarios, MoEGuard reduces recovery cost relative to checkpoint restart while maintaining validation loss, perplexity, gradient stability, token drop rate, and expert load balance. These results demonstrate that an expert-weighted staleness density is an effective abstraction for repairing runtime failures in distributed AI training software.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Mixture of Experts, fault tolerance, distributed training, checkpoint recovery, elastic training
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models have become a dominant architecture for frontier large language models, powering systems such as Mixtral~\cite{jiang2024mixtral}, DeepSeek-MoE~\cite{dai2024deepseekmoe}, and Qwen3-MoE~\cite{qwen3report,qwen25report}, because they decouple model capacity from per-token compute by routing each input through only a small subset of experts. Training these models at scale, however, is a long-running, multi-week, multi-thousand-GPU software process in which failures are routine rather than exceptional. Meta's Llama-3 training trace reports a failure roughly every 45\,minutes across a 16K-GPU cluster~\cite{dubey2024llama3}; MegaScale documents hundreds of distinct failure modes during a 10K-GPU production deployment~\cite{jiang2024megascale}; the OPT-175B logbook records weeks of human-in-the-loop intervention~\cite{zhang2022opt}. Because the dominant recovery mechanism in production is \emph{checkpoint restart}---reload the most recent globally consistent snapshot and replay the lost iterations---each failure event on a large MoE job typically consumes hundreds of seconds of checkpoint I/O plus hundreds of replayed iterations, costing thousands of GPU-seconds per event and, cumulatively, days of wall time over a training run.

A substantial body of fault-tolerant training systems has been developed to reduce this cost, but almost all of it targets dense models. CheckFreq~\cite{mohan2021checkfreq}, DeepFreeze~\cite{nicolae2020deepfreeze}, and Check-N-Run~\cite{eisenman2022check} shrink per-checkpoint cost via asynchronous and incremental writes; Gemini~\cite{wang2023gemini} replaces NVMe writes with in-memory replicas; Bamboo~\cite{thorpe2023bamboo} hides checkpointing behind redundancy on preemptible instances; Oobleck~\cite{jang2023oobleck} and ReCycle~\cite{gandhi2024recycle} avoid rolling back to any saved snapshot at all by reconfiguring pipeline schedules. None of these systems addresses MoE-specific cost asymmetries: the bulk of a MoE checkpoint is per-expert optimizer state, and the dominant Megatron-LM distributed checkpoint format (\texttt{torch\_dist}) requires every rank to participate in the load through a collective \texttt{all\_gather\_object}, which forbids single-rank selective restoration by construction. The only MoE-specific fault-tolerance system we are aware of, MoC-System~\cite{cai2024moc}, optimizes \emph{save-side} cost via Partial Experts Checkpointing but at recovery time still falls back to full global checkpoint restart---paying the full load + replay bill on every failure and leaving the recovery critical path essentially unchanged.

The gap is therefore a \emph{recovery-side} fault-tolerance framework that is structured around the heterogeneous state of MoE training---one that can repair a failed rank without rolling back the whole job, and do so in a way that is fast, model-quality-preserving, and stable across repeated failures. We present \textbf{MoEGuard}, such a framework, integrated into Megatron-LM~\cite{narayanan2021megatron}. The core technical idea exploits a structural asymmetry of distributed MoE training: dense parameters and routers are \emph{replicated} across the data-parallel (DP) dimension, while only the expert parameters and their optimizer state are EP-sharded. When a rank fails, the replacement rank therefore does not need to read dense state from disk at all---it can synchronize the current-step dense and router parameters directly from a healthy DP peer, and only the rank-local expert weights and expert optimizer state need to be loaded from the latest checkpoint shard. The intrinsic redundancy that the DP dimension already provides for free is, in other words, sufficient to repair the much larger replicated half of the state at memory-to-memory speed, leaving only the genuinely rank-unique experts on the disk path. A weights-first / optimizer-later two-phase protocol further overlaps optimizer-state restoration with the replacement rank's resumed forward/backward computation, so time-to-resume is bounded by a single weight transfer rather than by the full optimizer load.

We implement MoEGuard in Megatron-LM and evaluate it on Qwen3-30B-A3B~\cite{qwen3report} (48 layers, 128 routed experts, top-8 routing) on a 64-GPU H20-3e cluster with FineWeb~\cite{penedo2024fineweb} as the pre-training corpus. Compared to MoC-System on identical hardware and identical checkpoint gap, MoEGuard reduces end-to-end recovery wall time by roughly $4\times$ on single-rank failures and by $3\times$--$5\times$ across single-, repeated-, and concentrated-failure scenarios. Because every recovered run is paired with a NoFault baseline using identical seed, data order, checkpoint, and injected failure step~\cite{wohlin2012ese,arcuri2014hitchhiker}, we can attribute residual model-quality differences specifically to recovery rather than to seed noise: across all evaluated scenarios, post-recovery validation loss, perplexity, gradient norms, token drop rate, and expert load balance all stay within the failure-free $\pm 1\sigma$ band, i.e., model quality is essentially unaffected by recovery.

This paper makes the following contributions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A formulation of MoE training fault tolerance as a recovery-side problem distinct from save-side checkpoint optimization, and an articulation of why state-of-the-art dense-oriented systems (Bamboo, Oobleck, ReCycle, Gemini) and MoC-System leave this side unsolved (\S\ref{sec:background}).
    \item A DP-redundancy-driven hybrid recovery mechanism that synchronizes dense and router state from healthy DP peers at the current step and restores only the rank-local expert weights and optimizer state from per-rank checkpoint shards, together with a two-phase weights-first / optimizer-later protocol that further reduces time-to-resume below time-to-full-recovery (\S\ref{sec:moeguard}).
    \item A safe-point repair protocol that prevents partial optimizer commits during recovery, an expert-weighted staleness density $\Phi'(t)$ used as a runtime-checkable guard against accumulated quality risk over repeated hybrid recoveries, and structured recovery logs that make every recovery decision auditable (\S\ref{sec:moeguard}).
    \item An empirical evaluation against MoC-System and full checkpoint restart on Qwen3-30B-A3B, showing $3\times$--$5\times$ end-to-end recovery speedup while keeping model quality inside the failure-free $\pm 1\sigma$ band (\S\ref{sec:eval}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Background and Motivation}
\label{sec:background}

\subsection{Distributed MoE Training as Software}

A distributed MoE training job~\cite{lepikhin2021gshard,fedus2022switch,rajbhandari2022deepspeedmoe,hwang2023tutel,gale2023megablocks} is a long-running, stateful, multi-process program executing collective communication on tens to thousands of GPUs. Unlike dense Transformers, MoE training maintains a \emph{heterogeneous state} that we partition along its data-parallel (DP), tensor-parallel (TP), pipeline-parallel (PP), and expert-parallel (EP) axes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{Replicated state.} Dense layers, shared parameters, and router weights are replicated across every DP rank that owns the same PP/TP coordinate. At any iteration $t$ at least one healthy peer holds the current step's value.
    \item \emph{Sharded expert state.} Expert weights and their AdamW first/second moments are partitioned across EP ranks. In a $128$-expert, EP$=8$ layout, each rank exclusively owns a fixed subset of $128/8=16$ experts; no peer holds a current in-memory replica.
    \item \emph{Runtime metadata.} The expert directory, the dispatch topology used by all-to-all routing~\cite{he2022fastermoe,zhai2023smartmoe}, and the process-group view are derived from the rank layout and become stale the moment any rank is replaced.
\end{itemize}
This heterogeneity has a software-engineering consequence that does not arise in dense training: \emph{the same failure invalidates different state classes with different recovery semantics, but standard training runtimes treat all state as a single all-or-nothing checkpoint object.}

\subsection{Why Checkpoint Restart Becomes a Reliability Bottleneck}

The de facto recovery mechanism in production LLM training is checkpoint restart~\cite{narayanan2021megatron,dubey2024llama3,jiang2024megascale}. On a failure, the job reloads the most recent globally consistent snapshot and replays the lost iterations. Classical first-order analysis~\cite{young1974first,daly2006higher} shows that the optimal checkpoint interval scales as $\tau^{*}\!\approx\!\sqrt{2CM}$, where $C$ is the per-checkpoint cost and $M$ is the mean time between failures; with $M$ collapsing to tens of minutes on 10K-GPU clusters~\cite{dubey2024llama3,jiang2024megascale} the per-job cost of restart-only recovery grows quickly. Recent fault-tolerance work attacks the two factors of $\tau^{*}$ separately: CheckFreq~\cite{mohan2021checkfreq}, DeepFreeze~\cite{nicolae2020deepfreeze}, and Check-N-Run~\cite{eisenman2022check} shrink $C$ via asynchronous and incremental writes; Gemini~\cite{wang2023gemini} replaces NVMe writes with in-memory replicas; Bamboo~\cite{thorpe2023bamboo} hides $C$ behind redundancy on preemptible instances; Oobleck~\cite{jang2023oobleck} and ReCycle~\cite{gandhi2024recycle} reduce $C$ by reconfiguring pipeline schedules without rolling back to a saved snapshot at all. None of these systems, however, addresses the cost asymmetry that is specific to MoE: the bulk of a MoE checkpoint is per-expert optimizer state, and the dominant Megatron-LM distributed checkpoint format (\texttt{torch\_dist}) requires every rank to participate in the load through a collective \texttt{all\_gather\_object}, which forbids single-rank selective restoration by construction.

