\end{table}

\subsection{The Hidden Risk of Partial Recovery: Stale Expert State}

Once we admit hybrid recovery, the question shifts from ``how fast can we restart'' to ``what does the post-recovery training trajectory look like.'' Table~\ref{tab:state-provenance} captures the provenance asymmetry: replicated state can be reconstituted at the current step from a healthy peer, but EP-sharded expert state can only come from a checkpoint shard that is stale by the checkpoint gap $\Delta = t-c$. This stale expert state is not a binary defect---restored experts still produce valid forward/backward signals---but it perturbs the auxiliary-loss-driven balancing dynamics that MoE training depends on~\cite{shazeer2017outrageously,fedus2022switch,zoph2022stmoe,komatsuzaki2023sparse}. Two pathologies are particularly relevant:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \emph{Per-event staleness.} A single hybrid recovery exposes the experts owned by the failed rank to stale-by-$\Delta$ weights. As long as the affected fraction $|E_{\text{new}}|/N_{\text{expert}}$ stays small, the auxiliary load-balancing loss can reabsorb this staleness within the next few hundred iterations.
    \item \emph{Window-level staleness debt.} Because each rank owns a stable subset of experts, repeated hybrid recoveries---even distributed across different ranks---accumulate stale-by-$\Delta$ exposure across an increasingly large fraction of the expert population. No per-failure or per-rank metric (checkpoint gap, time since last failure, number of past failures) captures this accumulating risk.
\end{enumerate}

Existing fault-tolerant training systems do not expose either failure mode as an observable, much less as a controllable bound. Bamboo, Oobleck, ReCycle, and Varuna~\cite{athlur2022varuna} all reason about \emph{whether} training resumes, not about how far the post-recovery trajectory drifts from the failure-free one. The MoE-specific MoC-System~\cite{cai2024moc} reduces save-side cost via Partial Experts Checkpointing but still falls back to full checkpoint restart on recovery and provides no runtime guard on accumulated expert staleness.

\subsection{Software Engineering Requirements for Self-Healing MoE Training}
\label{sec:requirements}

Treating MoE recovery as a software engineering problem---rather than as a checkpoint-optimization or pipeline-reconfiguration problem---makes the missing properties precise. We borrow the vocabulary of self-adaptive and self-healing systems~\cite{kephart2003autonomic,garlan2004rainbow,salehie2009selfadaptive} to identify three requirements on which existing training runtimes are silent and to which MoEGuard is designed:

\begin{description}
    \item[(R1) Safe-point pre-conditions.] A repair invoked in the middle of forward/backward propagation or an in-flight optimizer step may commit parameter updates derived from invalid local state. The runtime must define an explicit safe repair point and gate optimizer commits against it.
    \item[(R2) Runtime-checkable specifications.] Partial repair must be governed by a quantitative, computable guard that bounds the staleness it injects into the cluster. The guard should be cheap enough to evaluate at every failure ($O(1)$ time) and expressive enough to distinguish single-event risk, cluster-wide accumulated risk, and per-event size; it should also have a meaningful cliff edge supported by data, not just a hand-tuned constant.
    \item[(R3) Traceable observability.] Every recovery decision must produce a structured record that links policy inputs, the selected path, and the downstream model-quality outcome. This is what makes ``did recovery succeed?'' a falsifiable question: in MoE training, a job that resumes with a degraded post-recovery loss is a recovery \emph{failure}, not a recovery success.
\end{description}

We use R1--R3 as the spine of the MoEGuard design in Section~\ref{sec:moeguard}: §\ref{sec:safepoint} instantiates R1 as safe-point repair, §\ref{sec:policy} instantiates R2 as the expert-weighted staleness density $\Phi'(t)$, and §\ref{sec:obs} instantiates R3 as structured recovery logs. The state-restoration mechanism (§\ref{sec:mechanism}) and the two-phase recovery protocol (§\ref{sec:twophase}) are how MoEGuard repairs state \emph{within} the contract that R2 defines.

\subsection{Distributed Checkpoint Formats in Megatron-LM}
\label{sec:ckpt-format}

Megatron-LM~\cite{narayanan2021megatron,korthikanti2023reducing} supports two checkpoint formats whose semantics determine whether hybrid recovery is feasible at all:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{torch} (legacy): each rank saves its own \texttt{.pt} file under \texttt{iter\_XXXXXXX/mp\_rank\_\{tp\}\_\{pp\}\_\{ep\}/\\model\_optim\_rng.pt}. These files can be loaded independently by each rank using \texttt{torch.load()}, which requires no collective communication---making them suitable for selective expert loading inside an asynchronous recovery callback.
    \item \texttt{torch\_dist} (default): uses distributed checkpointing with sharded state dicts and \texttt{metadata.json}. Loading requires \texttt{dist\_checkpointing.load()}, which internally calls \texttt{all\_gather\_object}---a collective operation requiring every rank to participate. This format is fundamentally incompatible with single-rank selective loading and deadlocks when called from a single rank's recovery thread.
\end{itemize}

This format constraint is a recurring theme in §\ref{sec:moeguard}: enabling MoE-aware hybrid recovery in Megatron-LM is not a tuning choice but a checkpoint-format choice, and MoEGuard's implementation in §\ref{sec:impl} commits to \texttt{torch} so that single-rank expert shards can be read collectively-free.

\section{MoEGuard}
\label{sec:moeguard}

We present MoEGuard in a single section organized around the three software-engineering requirements R1--R3 from §\ref{sec:requirements}. §\ref{sec:sysmodel} fixes the system model and fault assumptions. §\ref{sec:arch} gives the architecture overview. §\ref{sec:safepoint} instantiates R1 (safe-point pre-conditions) as a safe-point repair protocol that guards optimizer commits. §\ref{sec:policy} instantiates R2 (runtime-checkable specifications) as the expert-weighted staleness density $\Phi'(t)$ and the resulting decision policy. §\ref{sec:mechanism} and §\ref{sec:twophase} describe the recovery mechanisms governed by that specification. §\ref{sec:obs} instantiates R3 (traceable observability) as reintegration accounting and structured recovery logs. §\ref{sec:impl} describes the Megatron-LM realization. Throughout, we deliberately separate \emph{what is guaranteed} (the contract) from \emph{how it is achieved} (the mechanism), so that the recovery contract remains auditable independently of the implementation~\cite{garlan2004rainbow,salehie2009selfadaptive}.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{architecture.pdf}
\caption{MoEGuard runtime architecture. A failure detector triggers the safe-point repair controller (R1), which blocks unsafe optimizer commits. The expert-weighted-staleness guarded policy (R2) then selects between checkpoint restart and MoE-aware hybrid recovery using the checkpoint gap $\Delta$ and the cluster's running density $\Phi'(t)$. Hybrid recovery synchronizes dense, shared, and router state from healthy DP peers and restores the failed rank's expert weights and optimizer state from per-rank checkpoint shards through a weights-first / optimizer-later two-phase protocol. When a failure (or burst of concurrent failures) eliminates every healthy DP peer at the failed rank's PP/TP coordinate, hybrid recovery is by construction infeasible and MoEGuard transparently falls back to checkpoint restart. A reintegration manager refreshes the expert directory, dispatch topology, and process-group view; structured recovery logs (R3) feed both system metrics and model-quality metrics back to the policy.}
\label{fig:moeguard-overview}
\end{figure}

\subsection{System Model and Fault Assumptions}
\label{sec:sysmodel}

We consider a distributed MoE training job running with TP, PP, DP, and EP parallelism on $G$ GPUs. Each logical rank participates in NCCL/Gloo collectives and exclusively owns a fixed subset of $N_{\text{expert}}/\text{EP}$ MoE experts per MoE layer. A failure invalidates a rank when it crashes, becomes unreachable, or enters an invalid runtime state (e.g., NaN loss, CUDA error, watchdog timeout~\cite{jiang2024megascale}).

Let a failure occur on logical rank $r$ at training step $t$, with $c$ the latest checkpoint step before the failure. We define the \emph{checkpoint gap}
\begin{equation}
    \Delta \;=\; t - c.
\end{equation}
In checkpoint restart $\Delta$ determines the number of iterations replayed; in hybrid recovery $\Delta$ determines how stale the restored expert state is. We make four assumptions consistent with standard fault-tolerant training settings~\cite{thorpe2023bamboo,jang2023oobleck,wang2023gemini}: (i)~the failure detector~\cite{jiang2024megascale} reports rank failures in a fail-stop model (no Byzantine ranks~\cite{dubey2024llama3}); (ii)~at least one checkpoint exists for $c\geq 0$; (iii)~for every failed rank $r$, at least one healthy DP peer holds the current-step replica of $r$'s dense/router state at the same PP/TP coordinate; (iv)~the failed rank can be replaced or restarted on a hot-spare GPU within the training job's process-manager infrastructure. Assumption (iii) is a structural pre-condition of hybrid recovery: when a failure (or a burst of concurrent failures) eliminates every healthy DP peer at the failed rank's PP/TP coordinate, no surviving rank holds the current-step replica, the assumption is violated, and MoEGuard's policy module deterministically falls back to checkpoint restart (§\ref{sec:policy}). Concurrent multi-rank failures, corrupted checkpoints, silent data corruption~\cite{chowdhery2023palm}, and bit-flips are otherwise outside the current scope and are deferred to §\ref{sec:threats}.

\subsection{Architectural Overview}
\label{sec:arch}

Fig.~\ref{fig:moeguard-overview} shows the runtime architecture. MoEGuard consists of five cooperating components: a \emph{failure detector} (delegated to the existing watchdog and process-manager infrastructure), a \emph{safe-point repair controller}, an \emph{expert-weighted-staleness guarded policy module}, a \emph{MoE-aware state restoration module} (with two-phase recovery), and a \emph{reintegration manager}. On a detected failure, MoEGuard executes four steps. \textbf{Step 1.} The safe-point repair controller blocks the current iteration from committing an unsafe optimizer update (R1). \textbf{Step 2.} The policy module computes the checkpoint gap $\Delta$, the projected $\Phi'(t)$, and the peer-availability predicate, and deterministically selects between MoE-aware hybrid recovery and full checkpoint restart (R2); when no healthy DP peer is available the policy must select restart. \textbf{Step 3.} It executes the selected recovery path. \textbf{Step 4.} The reintegration manager refreshes runtime metadata and brings the repaired rank back into training under a controlled state machine, with every transition emitted to the structured log (R3).

\subsection{Safe-Point Repair (R1)}
\label{sec:safepoint}

A rank failure may occur at any point in the iteration---forward propagation, backward propagation, gradient all-reduce, or the optimizer step itself. Directly repairing the failed rank without controlling the training boundary can commit parameter updates derived from stale gradients or partially-reduced tensors. MoEGuard therefore enforces a \emph{safe-point repair protocol} as its R1 contract: when rank $r$ fails at step $t$, the controller atomically (i)~marks $r$ and its local experts as \texttt{RECOVERING}, (ii)~installs an optimizer commit guard that blocks the current iteration's \texttt{optimizer.step()} on every rank, and (iii)~marks the iteration as \texttt{DISCARD}. The guard is a pre-condition check inserted before the AdamW update; it is the explicit invariant ``no optimizer commit may use gradients computed under \texttt{RECOVERING}''. The unsafe iteration is dropped---never partially committed---and training resumes either from the safe repair point (hybrid path) or from the last checkpoint (restart path). The simplicity of this contract is intentional: MoEGuard does not attempt to reconcile arbitrary partially-committed updates, and therefore avoids the soundness pitfalls of mid-iteration rollback observed in earlier resilient-training systems~\cite{thorpe2023bamboo,jang2023oobleck}. For empirical evaluation, this contract is exercised by paired NoFault / Recovered runs with a controlled rank failure (see §\ref{sec:eval}), following standard empirical-software-engineering protocols for reproducible distributed-systems experiments~\cite{wohlin2012ese,arcuri2014hitchhiker}.

\subsection{Expert-Weighted Staleness Density: A Runtime-Checkable Recovery Specification (R2)}
\label{sec:policy}

R2 demands a quantitative, $O(1)$-computable guard that bounds the staleness injected into the cluster. Because each logical rank owns a fixed subset of MoE experts (§\ref{sec:sysmodel}), repeated hybrid recoveries---even when distributed across different ranks---progressively leave more experts in a stale-by-$\Delta$ state. What determines cluster-level quality risk is therefore not just \emph{how many iterations} of staleness a single event injects, but \emph{how large a fraction of the expert population} has been exposed to staleness inside a recent training window, weighted by per-event magnitude. This is precisely the quantity captured by the expert-weighted staleness density.

\textbf{Definition.} Let $\mathcal{H}_W(t)$ denote past hybrid-recovery events in the sliding window $[t-W,t)$. For each historical event $h\in\mathcal{H}_W(t)$ we log the size of its restored expert set $|E_h|$ and its gap $\Delta_h$. For a new failure at step $t$ with checkpoint gap $\Delta$ that would restore $|E_{\text{new}}|$ experts, the projected expert-weighted staleness density is
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:phi-prime}
    \Phi'(t) \;=\; \frac{\displaystyle\sum_{h \in \mathcal{H}_W(t)} |E_h|\cdot \Delta_h \;+\; |E_{\text{new}}|\cdot \Delta}{N_{\text{expert}}\cdot W},
\end{equation}
where $N_{\text{expert}}$ is the total number of MoE experts in the model. The numerator is the ``expert$\,\times\,$iteration'' staleness debt accumulated in the current window; the denominator $N_{\text{expert}}\cdot W$ is the maximum staleness debt the model could possibly accumulate if every expert were stale by every iteration of $W$. $\Phi'(t)$ is therefore dimensionless and lies in $[0,1]$. Maintaining it requires only a constant-size sliding-window aggregate (sum of $|E_h|\Delta_h$ over the window), so the evaluation cost per failure is $O(1)$.

\textbf{Policy.} MoEGuard uses three thresholds---$\Delta_{\min}$, $\Delta_{\max}$, and $\Phi_{\max}$---and one structural predicate $\textsc{PeerAvail}(r,t)$ that is true iff at least one healthy DP peer holds the current-step replica of failed rank $r$'s dense/router state (assumption (iii) of §\ref{sec:sysmodel}). The decision function is
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:policy}
    \pi(t) \;=\;
    \begin{cases}
        \textsc{Hybrid}, & \textsc{PeerAvail}(r,t) \;\wedge\; \Delta \geq \Delta_{\min} \;\wedge\; \Delta \leq \Delta_{\max} \;\wedge\; \Phi'(t) \leq \Phi_{\max}, \\
        \textsc{Restart}, & \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
Each clause encodes a distinct safety condition. $\textsc{PeerAvail}(r,t)$ rules out the structurally infeasible case where no current-step source exists---i.e., when a failure (or a burst of concurrent failures) has eliminated every healthy DP peer at the failed rank's PP/TP coordinate, so no surviving rank holds the current-step replica of dense/router state; in these cases hybrid recovery is impossible by construction and the policy is forced to \textsc{Restart}. $\Delta\!\geq\!\Delta_{\min}$ enforces a 1.2$\times$ end-to-end speedup precondition (§\ref{sec:rq1}); $\Delta\!\leq\!\Delta_{\max}$ caps per-event staleness so that no single hybrid event exposes the affected expert subset to more than one save-interval of drift; $\Phi'(t)\!\leq\!\Phi_{\max}$ caps cluster-wide accumulated staleness in $W$ so that repeated hybrid recoveries cannot exceed the empirical cliff edge derived in §\ref{sec:eval}. When the policy denies hybrid recovery, MoEGuard falls back to checkpoint restart, which resets the global expert state to a fully consistent snapshot and zeroes out the staleness debt for events outside the new exposure window.

\textbf{Why this is a runtime-checkable specification rather than a heuristic.} The three thresholds in (\ref{eq:policy}) are not free hyperparameters tuned to a benchmark. $\Delta_{\min}$ is derived in §\ref{sec:rq1} from the measured cost ratio $T_{\text{load}}\!:\!T_{\text{hybrid}}\!:\!T_{\text{iter}}$ on the current cluster; $\Delta_{\max}$ is set by the checkpoint save interval, which itself is bounded by Young/Daly~\cite{young1974first,daly2006higher}; $\Phi_{\max}$ is set in §\ref{sec:eval} from a $3\!\times\!4$ multi-rank burst sweep that exhibits a clean cliff edge separating cells inside and outside the failure-free $\pm 1\sigma$ band. The $\textsc{PeerAvail}$ predicate is a binary structural check on the live process-group view, not a tuned parameter. The specification is therefore traceable to measurable system properties and to a quality-driven empirical threshold, in line with the runtime-verification view advocated for self-adaptive systems~\cite{kephart2003autonomic,garlan2004rainbow,salehie2009selfadaptive}.

\subsection{MoE-Aware Hybrid State Restoration (Mechanism)}
\label{sec:mechanism}

When the policy admits hybrid recovery, MoEGuard reconstitutes state according to the provenance asymmetry of Table~\ref{tab:state-provenance}. Different state classes have different fresh sources, and MoEGuard reads each from its appropriate source rather than treating the checkpoint as a single all-or-nothing object. We assume here that the structural pre-condition $\textsc{PeerAvail}(r,t)$ of (\ref{eq:policy}) holds; otherwise---i.e., when a failure (or burst of concurrent failures) has knocked out every healthy DP peer at the failed rank's PP/TP coordinate---no current-step peer source exists, hybrid recovery is by construction infeasible, and MoEGuard transparently falls back to checkpoint restart.

\textbf{Dense, shared, and router state.} The replacement rank synchronizes the latest parameters (and, where DP-replicated, the optimizer state) from a healthy DP peer that shares the same TP/PP coordinate. In pipeline-parallel training~\cite{narayanan2021megatron} the synchronization source is a healthy rank in a different DP pipeline at the same pipeline-stage coordinate as the failed rank. The transfer is a single peer-to-peer broadcast over NCCL/Gloo and completes in tens of milliseconds for the relevant tensors; importantly, it produces a state that reflects the \emph{current} training step, not the latest checkpoint.

\textbf{Expert weights and expert optimizer state.} The replacement rank reads the experts owned by the failed rank from the latest distributed checkpoint shard. The shard is a per-rank \texttt{.pt} file under
\begin{equation}
    \texttt{iter\_XXXXXXX/mp\_rank\_\{tp:02d\}\_\{pp:03d\}\_\{ep:03d\}/model\_optim\_rng.pt},
\end{equation}
loadable independently with \texttt{torch.load()} and \emph{no collective communication}. With pipeline parallelism, global MoE layer indices are remapped to PP-stage-local indices via
\begin{equation}
    \text{local\_idx} \;=\; \text{layer\_id} \;-\; (\text{pp\_rank} \cdot L + 1),
\end{equation}
where $L$ is the number of layers per PP stage. As discussed in §\ref{sec:ckpt-format}, this single-rank loading path is structurally unavailable in the default \texttt{torch\_dist} format because \texttt{dist\_checkpointing.load()} requires \texttt{all\_gather\_object}; choosing the \texttt{torch} format is therefore part of the hybrid-recovery contract, not a tuning knob.

\textbf{Provenance of the recovered state.} The recovered rank's state is then \emph{mixed by construction}: dense/shared/router state reflects step $t$, while the failed rank's experts and expert optimizer state are stale by exactly $\Delta$ iterations relative to step $t$. This controlled staleness is exactly the quantity tracked by $\Phi'(t)$ in (\ref{eq:phi-prime}). The contract (R2) bounds the risk; the mechanism (this subsection) realizes the repair within that bound.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{hybrid_recovery.png}
\caption{MoE-aware hybrid state recovery. \emph{(1)}~A failure invalidates rank $r$. \emph{(2)}~The replacement rank runs two parallel paths: peer-to-peer pull synchronizes dense/router weights (and DP-replicated optimizer state) from a healthy peer at the same PP-stage coordinate; per-rank checkpoint load restores rank-$r$'s expert weights and expert optimizer state from the latest shard. \emph{(3)}~After the reintegration manager refreshes expert directory, dispatch topology, and process-group metadata, $r$ rejoins training without full-job rollback. The combined state is fresh on dense/router and stale by at most $\Delta$ iterations on experts; this staleness is the quantity bounded by $\Phi'(t)$.}
\label{fig:hybrid-recovery}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Two-Phase Weights-First / Optimizer-Later Protocol}
\label{sec:twophase}

Expert optimizer states are large (AdamW first/second moments roughly double the weight bytes) and read-bandwidth-bound. To shorten the interval between failure detection and resumed forward/backward execution, MoEGuard supports a two-phase recovery protocol.

\textbf{Phase A (weights first).} MoEGuard restores only the state needed for forward and backward execution: dense and router weights from healthy peers, and rank-$r$'s expert weights from the latest checkpoint shard. Once these weights and the runtime metadata (§\ref{sec:obs}) are in place, the repaired rank rejoins training under an \emph{update barrier} on the affected experts: forward/backward computations involving those experts proceed, but their gradients are buffered rather than applied.

\textbf{Phase B (optimizer later).} In parallel with Phase-A training, MoEGuard restores the optimizer states---DP-replicated dense/router moments from the healthy peer, and rank-$r$'s expert moments from the checkpoint shard. When all optimizer state is attached, the update barrier is released and the buffered gradients are applied via a single AdamW step; normal update semantics resume.

\textbf{Time-to-resume vs.\ time-to-full-recovery.} This protocol makes a distinction that single-phase restart cannot: \emph{time-to-resume} ($T_{\text{TTR}}$) is the wall time from failure detection to the rank's first post-recovery training iteration; \emph{time-to-full-recovery} ($T_{\text{TTFR}}$) is the wall time until normal optimizer update semantics are restored. By overlapping Phase B with Phase-A forward/backward execution, MoEGuard pushes $T_{\text{TTR}}$ down to the cost of weight transfer alone, bounded by one training iteration (Table~\ref{tab:single}, §\ref{sec:rq2}). The update barrier preserves the safe-point invariant of §\ref{sec:safepoint} throughout the overlap window.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{two_phase_recovery.png}
\caption{Weights-first / optimizer-later two-phase recovery. \emph{(1)~Failure}: a rank crashes. \emph{(2)~Phase 1 (weights first)}: dense/router weights pulled from a healthy peer; expert weights loaded from the per-rank checkpoint shard; training resumes under an update barrier on the affected experts. \emph{(3)~Phase 2 (optimizer later)}: dense/router optimizer state synchronized from the peer and expert optimizer state restored from checkpoint, while the barrier blocks unsafe optimizer commits on the still-uninitialized expert slots. \emph{(4)~Fully recovered}: barrier released, normal update semantics restored. The shaded gap between Phase~1 and Phase~2 is exactly $T_{\text{TTFR}} - T_{\text{TTR}}$.}
\label{fig:two-phase-recovery}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Reintegration and Structured Recovery Logs (R3)}
\label{sec:obs}

\textbf{Reintegration as a state machine.} State restoration alone is insufficient: routing tokens to a rank whose expert directory, dispatch topology~\cite{hwang2023tutel,he2022fastermoe}, or process-group view is stale will silently produce wrong outputs. MoEGuard's reintegration manager moves the repaired rank through an explicit state machine---\texttt{RECOVERING} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{REPAIRED\_NOT\_ROUTED} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{ROUTED\_BARRIER} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{HEALTHY}---and only transitions to the next state once the corresponding metadata is refreshed and verified. The rank does not receive normal expert traffic until \texttt{ROUTED\_BARRIER}, and the update barrier is held until \texttt{HEALTHY}. This makes premature reintegration impossible by construction.

\textbf{Structured recovery logs (R3).} Every recovery event produces a structured record with: failure cause, failed rank, checkpoint step $c$, checkpoint gap $\Delta$, $|E_{\text{new}}|$, the running components of $\Phi'(t)$ (window aggregate, new contribution, projected value), the $\textsc{PeerAvail}$ predicate, the selected path, the decision reason, dense synchronization time, expert restoration time, optimizer attach time, replay time (for restart), reintegration time, and the rank's state-machine trace. These records have two functions. First, they make recovery decisions \emph{explainable}: every selected path traces back to policy inputs and thresholds (\ref{eq:policy}). Second, they enable \emph{post-hoc correlation} with training-quality metrics---validation loss, perplexity, gradient norm, gradient clipping rate, token drop rate, router auxiliary loss, and expert-load CV. This correlation is what turns ``did recovery succeed?'' into a falsifiable question, and is the operational backbone of the paired-run methodology used throughout §\ref{sec:eval}~\cite{wohlin2012ese,arcuri2014hitchhiker}.

\subsection{Implementation in Megatron-LM}
\label{sec:impl}

MoEGuard is implemented as $\sim$4.2K lines of Python integrated with Megatron-LM~\cite{narayanan2021megatron,korthikanti2023reducing}. Three implementation decisions deserve emphasis. \textbf{(i)~Checkpoint format.} MoEGuard commits to the \texttt{torch} format on save so that the recovery path can call \texttt{torch.load()} on a single rank without collective communication; this is a hard requirement, not a tuning choice (§\ref{sec:ckpt-format}). \textbf{(ii)~PP-aware layer remapping.} The global-to-local MoE layer index map of §\ref{sec:mechanism} is computed once at startup and cached, so the recovery hot path performs only $O(\text{layers per stage})$ lookups. \textbf{(iii)~Update barrier as a hook.} The Phase-A update barrier is implemented as a pre-step hook on the AdamW optimizer that masks gradient application for the affected expert parameter groups, sharing the same enforcement point as the safe-point optimizer commit guard (§\ref{sec:safepoint}). All three decisions are observable from the structured recovery logs (§\ref{sec:obs}); none of them is hidden inside the implementation.

\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

This section evaluates whether MoEGuard can recover distributed MoE training failures efficiently while preserving training stability. Our evaluation answers the following research questions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ1.} Does MoEGuard select the intended recovery path under different checkpoint gaps and expert-weighted staleness levels?
    \item \textbf{RQ2.} How much recovery cost does MoEGuard reduce compared with checkpoint restart?
    \item \textbf{RQ3.} Does hybrid recovery affect training quality or stability?
    \item \textbf{RQ4.} How does MoEGuard behave under repeated failures, especially concentrated failures on the same rank?
    \item \textbf{RQ5.} What is the runtime overhead of MoEGuard during failure-free training?
    \item \textbf{RQ6.} Which design components contribute most to MoEGuard's benefit?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We implement MoEGuard in Megatron-LM. Unless otherwise specified, experiments use distributed MoE training with tensor parallel size 1, pipeline parallel size 8, expert parallel size 8, and data parallel replicas across pipeline copies. The main experiments use 64 H20-3e GPUs, organized as 8 nodes with 8 GPUs per node. We additionally use 128 H20-3e GPUs for scalability experiments. Each H20-3e GPU is an Hopper-architecture data-center accelerator with 141\,GB HBM3e memory and 900\,GB/s NVLink interconnect, optimized for large-model training and high-throughput collective communication.

We use the \emph{Qwen3-30B-A3B} sparse MoE architecture as our reference model. Qwen3-30B-A3B has 30B total parameters with approximately 3B activated per token, organized as 48 transformer layers, 128 routed experts per MoE layer, and top-8 routing. This configuration represents a realistic production-scale MoE training workload. Tokenization uses the \emph{Qwen2Tokenizer}, a byte-level BPE tokenizer with a 151{,}936-token vocabulary shared across the Qwen2/Qwen3 model family.

The default model configuration is shown in Table~\ref{tab:config}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Default Training Configuration}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Layers & 48 \\
Hidden size & 2048 \\
Attention heads & 32 \\
Query groups & 4 \\
Sequence length & 4096 \\
Global batch size & 64 \\
Precision & BF16 \\
Number of experts & 128 \\
MoE top-$k$ & 8 \\
MoE FFN hidden size & 768 \\
Router load balancing & auxiliary loss \\
Auxiliary loss coefficient & $1 \times 10^{-3}$ \\
Optimizer & AdamW \\
Learning rate & $1 \times 10^{-4}$ \\
Minimum learning rate & $1 \times 10^{-5}$ \\
LR schedule & cosine \\
Weight decay & 0.1 \\
Gradient clipping & 1.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:config}
\end{center}
\end{table}

We use a 4B-token subset of the \emph{FineWeb} corpus~\cite{penedo2024fineweb}, processed into Megatron indexed format using the Qwen2Tokenizer. FineWeb is a large-scale, deduplicated, and quality-filtered web-text dataset derived from CommonCrawl, widely adopted as a high-quality pretraining corpus for modern LLMs. The 4B-token subset provides a sufficiently large training distribution to ensure that recovery experiments are not bottlenecked by data repetition. For quality evaluation, we reserve a held-out validation split and report validation loss and validation perplexity every 1{,}000 iterations. Failure-recovery experiments use paired runs: the baseline and MoEGuard runs use the same seed, data order, checkpoint, model configuration, and injected failure step.

In our cluster, a normal iteration takes approximately 10--13 seconds, while loading a distributed checkpoint takes approximately 170--300 seconds depending on checkpoint size and storage contention. These measured values are used only to choose practical experimental ranges; MoEGuard's policy itself uses checkpoint gap and the expert-weighted staleness density $\Phi'(t)$.

\textbf{Pre-experiment: system timing characterization.} Before the main fault-injection experiments, we run a pre-experiment on the same 64-GPU cluster to characterize the steady-state cost of normal training, checkpoint save/load, and hybrid recovery. Each value is averaged over multiple runs of the Qwen3-30B-A3B configuration in Table~\ref{tab:config} with \texttt{save-interval}~$=200$. Table~\ref{tab:pre_timing} summarizes the measured costs.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Pre-experiment: Measured System Timing (64 H20-3e GPUs, Qwen3-30B-A3B)}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Operation} & \textbf{MoEGuard} & \textbf{Baseline} \\
\midrule
per iteration            & 10{,}259.5\,ms & 10{,}309\,ms \\
save-checkpoint          & 32{,}796.9\,ms & 33{,}529\,ms \\
load-checkpoint          & ---            & 35{,}951\,ms \\
hybrid-recovery overhead & 28{,}790.8\,ms & ---           \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:pre_timing}
\end{center}
\end{table}

Three observations follow from Table~\ref{tab:pre_timing}. First, MoEGuard's per-iteration cost (10.26\,s) is statistically indistinguishable from the baseline (10.31\,s), confirming that the safe-point repair instrumentation, $\Phi'(t)$ accounting, and recovery hooks add no measurable steady-state overhead during failure-free training. Second, the per-checkpoint save cost is $\sim$33\,s for both systems, which is consistent with the 30--60\,s range used in our save-interval analysis below and yields a checkpointing overhead of $33/(200\times 10.3) \approx 1.6\%$ at \texttt{save-interval}~$=200$. Third, MoEGuard's hybrid-recovery overhead (28.8\,s) is substantially smaller than a full checkpoint reload (35.95\,s in the baseline) and avoids replaying up to 200 lost iterations ($\sim$2{,}060\,s of GPU work in the worst case); the gap is even larger when including iteration replay, which we report in Section~\ref{sec:rq2}. These pre-experiment numbers are used only to ground the experimental ranges and overhead claims; all subsequent RQs report end-to-end metrics from paired fault-injection runs.

\textbf{Pre-experiment: baseline training-noise floor.} To establish a reference for what counts as a statistically meaningful change in model quality after recovery, we additionally characterize the run-to-run variance of \emph{failure-free} training. We launch \textbf{10 independent NoFault runs} of 600 iterations each, using the configuration in Table~\ref{tab:config} but with different random seeds and data shuffling orders. At iteration 600 we measure validation loss on the held-out FineWeb split. The 10 measured eval-loss values are:
\begin{center}
4.8118,\ 4.8674,\ 4.8528,\ 4.8199,\ 4.8456,\ 4.8686,\ 4.8739,\ 4.8903,\ 4.8570,\ 4.8556.
\end{center}
The summary statistics are reported in Table~\ref{tab:pre_noise}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Pre-experiment: Baseline Eval-Loss Variability across 10 Independent NoFault Runs (600 iterations, Qwen3-30B-A3B)}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
\textbf{Statistic} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Number of runs                 & 10 \\
Iterations per run             & 600 \\
Mean eval loss $\mu$           & 4.854 \\
Standard deviation $\sigma$    & $\approx 0.024$ \\
Min eval loss                  & 4.8199 \\
Max eval loss                  & 4.8903 \\
Range (max $-$ min)            & 0.070 \\
Coefficient of variation       & $\approx 0.5\%$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:pre_noise}
\end{center}
\end{table}

This baseline noise floor has two implications for our recovery evaluation. First, with $\sigma \approx 0.024$ the 95\% confidence interval on the mean (using $\pm 1.96\sigma/\sqrt{n}$, $n=10$) is roughly $\pm 0.015$, so any post-recovery eval-loss difference smaller than $\sim$0.015--0.024 is within the natural seed/data-order variability of failure-free training and cannot be attributed to recovery damage. Second, this floor justifies our use of \emph{paired} runs (same seed, same data order, same checkpoint, same injected failure step) in subsequent RQs: pairing controls for the dominant source of noise observed here, so that any residual gap between MoEGuard and Restart/NoFault on validation loss, perplexity, gradient norm, token drop rate, or expert-load CV can be interpreted as a recovery-induced effect rather than a seed effect. We re-use $\mu = 4.854$, $\sigma \approx 0.024$ as the reference NoFault distribution at iteration 600 throughout Section~\ref{sec:rq3}.

\textbf{Checkpoint save interval.} Unless otherwise specified, all experiments save a full distributed checkpoint every \textbf{200 iterations} (\texttt{--save-interval 200} in Megatron-LM). We selected this value based on three considerations grounded in prior work and our cluster characterization. First, classical checkpoint-frequency analysis by Young~\cite{young1974first} and Daly~\cite{daly2006higher} shows that the optimal checkpoint interval $\tau^{*} \approx \sqrt{2 \cdot C \cdot M}$, where $C$ is the per-checkpoint cost and $M$ is the mean time between failures. With our measured per-checkpoint write cost of roughly 30--60\,s on shared storage and an LLM-training MTBF on the order of a few hours per job (consistent with the 45-minute average failure interval reported for Meta's 16K-GPU Llama-3 training run~\cite{dubey2024llama3} and the failure traces analyzed by MegaScale~\cite{jiang2024megascale}), Young/Daly's formula yields an optimal interval in the range of 100--300 iterations at our 10--13\,s per-iteration speed. Second, this range matches the operating points adopted by recent large-scale LLM training systems and fault-tolerance frameworks: Megatron-LM's reference recipes~\cite{narayanan2021megatron} use $\sim$100--500 iterations, CheckFreq~\cite{mohan2021checkfreq} advocates frequent fine-grained checkpoints with overhead $<$5\%, Bamboo~\cite{thorpe2023bamboo} and Oobleck~\cite{jang2023oobleck} report checkpoint cadences in the same regime, and Gemini~\cite{wang2023gemini} explicitly studies sub-iteration to few-hundred-iteration intervals for in-memory checkpointing. Third, 200 iterations is large enough to amortize the $\sim$30--60\,s save cost (overhead $<$3\% of wall-clock time at our iteration speed) while small enough that the worst-case checkpoint gap $\Delta$ stays well within the safe single-recovery region characterized in Section~\ref{sec:rq3}, so that hybrid recovery introduces at most $\sim$200 iterations of expert staleness per failure---comfortably below the $\Phi_{\max}=10^{-2}$ expert-weighted staleness bound of Section~\ref{sec:policy}. We therefore fix \texttt{save-interval}~$=200$ as the default and treat it as a representative production-scale operating point rather than a free hyperparameter.

\subsection{Compared Systems}\label{sec:compared}

We compare MoEGuard with the following baselines.

\textbf{NoFault.} Normal training without injected failures. This run provides the reference training trajectory.

\textbf{Restart.} Standard checkpoint restart. After failure, all ranks restore from the latest checkpoint and replay lost iterations.

\textbf{AlwaysHybrid.} Always use hybrid recovery whenever a failure occurs, ignoring gap, $\Delta_{\max}$, and the expert-weighted staleness density.

\textbf{FixedGap.} Use hybrid recovery when the checkpoint gap is above a fixed threshold, without tracking the cluster's expert-weighted staleness density.

\textbf{MoEGuard-w/o-Exposure.} MoEGuard without the expert-weighted staleness guard. This ablation uses only $\Delta_{\min}$ and $\Delta_{\max}$.

\textbf{MoEGuard-w/o-OptLater.} MoEGuard without weights-first, optimizer-later recovery. Expert weights and optimizer states must both be restored before the rank resumes training.

\textbf{MoEGuard.} The full system with safe-point repair, expert-weighted-staleness guarded policy, MoE-aware hybrid state restoration, two-phase recovery, and reintegration management.

\textbf{MoC-System (emulated).} An accuracy-equivalent emulation of MoC-System's Partial Experts Checkpointing (PEC)~\cite{cai2024moc} implemented as a non-invasive overlay on MoEGuard, controlled by the single environment switch \texttt{BSR\_MOC\_PEC\_EMULATE}. Because MoC-System's reference implementation is not publicly available, we obtain a byte-identical accuracy comparison without re-implementing its full system stack:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{Save path is unchanged.} MoEGuard continues to write the full $N$-expert checkpoint at every \texttt{save-interval}. A sidecar metadata file (\texttt{moc\_pec\_metadata.json}) records, for each save round, the contiguous $K_{\text{pec}}$ experts that PEC's round-robin schedule would have written as \emph{fresh} and, for every other expert, the earlier \texttt{iter\_*} directory that holds its last-fresh version.
    \item \emph{Restore path is intercepted.} Before \texttt{restore\_expert\_weights} runs, an overlay rewrites each non-fresh expert entry's source \texttt{checkpoint\_dir} to the historical directory chosen by the round-robin schedule. Because the older checkpoint physically exists (we never deleted it), the bytes loaded into the model are exactly what a faithful MoC-System implementation would have loaded.
    \item \emph{Hybrid recovery is disabled} for this configuration: dense and router state fall back to \texttt{CHECKPOINT\_RESTART}, matching MoC-System's restore behavior. Two-phase weights-first / optimizer-later recovery is also disabled.
\end{itemize}
With $K_{\text{pec}}=16$ and $N=128$ experts, the emulation reproduces the operating point reported in the MoC-System paper for PLT~$\approx 3.75\%$. Our recovery-time numbers for MoC-System are not measured from this overlay (which still writes the full checkpoint, so its wall-clock save cost does not reflect PEC's savings); instead we use the most favorable published recovery latency from the MoC-System paper as a conservative upper bound on its advantage. See Section~\ref{sec:threats} for details.

\subsection{Policy Parameters}

MoEGuard uses three thresholds: $\Delta_{\min}$, $\Delta_{\max}$, and $\Phi_{\max}$. Our default values are shown in Table~\ref{tab:policy}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Default Policy Parameters}
\label{tab:policy}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.1}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l p{0.46\columnwidth} l@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Param.} & \textbf{Meaning} & \textbf{Default} \\
\midrule
$\Delta_{\min}$    & min gap for hybrid recovery                           & 1\,iter           \\
$\Delta_{\max}$    & max single-event gap for hybrid recovery              & 200\,iters        \\
$\Phi_{\max}$      & max expert-weighted staleness density in $W$          & $1\!\times\!10^{-2}$ \\
$W$                & exposure window                                       & 20\,000\,iters    \\
$N_{\text{expert}}$ & total MoE experts in model                           & 128               \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

The default $\Delta_{\min}$ is derived from a \emph{1.2$\times$ end-to-end speedup} requirement: MoEGuard only switches from checkpoint restart to hybrid recovery if the full restart cost---checkpoint load plus iteration replay---is at least 1.2$\times$ the hybrid-recovery cost on the same cluster. Using the measured values from Table~\ref{tab:pre_timing} ($T_{\text{load}}=35.951$\,s, $T_{\text{iter}}=10.309$\,s, $T_{\text{hybrid}}=28.7908$\,s), this becomes
\begin{equation}
    T_{\text{load}} + \Delta \cdot T_{\text{iter}} \;\geq\; 1.2 \cdot T_{\text{hybrid}},
\end{equation}
which simplifies to $\Delta \geq (1.2 \cdot 28.7908 - 35.951)/10.309 = -0.142$. In other words, on our cluster the checkpoint-load cost alone (35.95\,s) is already $\sim$1.25$\times$ the hybrid-recovery cost (28.79\,s), so the 1.2$\times$ inequality is satisfied for every $\Delta \geq 1$. The smallest non-trivial gap that still admits hybrid recovery is therefore $\Delta_{\min} = 1$\,iter, i.e., the threshold is in practice \emph{non-binding} under our measured timings: whenever a failure is detected, hybrid recovery is already at least 1.2$\times$ faster than checkpoint restart. We keep $\Delta_{\min}$ as a first-class policy knob, however, because it acts as a \emph{defensive guard} for robustness: on clusters with much faster checkpoint loading (e.g., NVMe-backed or in-memory checkpoints~\cite{wang2023gemini}) the inequality can flip for small $\Delta$, and operators can raise $\Delta_{\min}$ to avoid switching to hybrid when restart would actually be cheaper. Throughout our experiments we therefore report $\Delta_{\min}=1$, but the mechanism itself remains in the policy.

The default $\Delta_{\max} = 200$ iterations matches the checkpoint save interval (Section~\ref{sec:eval}), which is itself the natural upper bound on any single-failure gap: by construction no failure can be more than \texttt{save-interval} iterations away from the latest checkpoint. $\Delta_{\max}$ is therefore non-binding on a single failure within one save interval, and is included as a defensive guard for operating regimes that disable or stretch the save interval.

The default $\Phi_{\max} = 1\times 10^{-2}$ bounds the expert-weighted staleness density that the cluster can accumulate inside the exposure window $W = 20000$ before MoEGuard falls back to checkpoint restart. The value is set from a $3\times 4$ multi-rank burst sweep (Table~\ref{tab:multi_fault}, detailed below): in the seven cells with $\Phi'(t)\leq 10^{-2}$ the post-recovery loss stays inside the baseline $\pm 1\sigma$ band, while in all five cells with $\Phi'(t)>10^{-2}$ the deviation rises to $1.21$--$1.98\sigma_{\text{base}}$ and starts touching the baseline $\pm 2\sigma$ edge. To put $\Phi_{\max}=10^{-2}$ on a concrete scale: with $N_{\text{expert}}=128$, $W=20000$, $|E_{\text{new}}| = 16$ (a single failed rank in our PP$=8$, EP$=8$ layout owns $128/8 = 16$ experts), and $\Delta \leq \Delta_{\max} = 200$, a single-rank hybrid-recovery event contributes at most $16\cdot 200 / (128\cdot 20000) = 1.25\times 10^{-3}$ to $\Phi'(t)$, so $\Phi_{\max}=10^{-2}$ admits roughly eight worst-case single-rank hybrid recoveries per window before falling back to restart---enough headroom for realistic failure rates while still cutting off the multi-rank burst regime where Table~\ref{tab:multi_fault} shows quality degradation.

\textbf{Single-rank validation: $\Phi_{\max}$ is non-binding at the low-density end of $\Phi'(t)$.} We first probe the lower end of the $\Phi'(t)$ axis with a 50-run single-rank fault-injection sweep on the same Qwen3-30B-A3B configuration (PP$=8$, EP$=8$, 64 H20-3e GPUs). For each run we inject one single-rank failure at a distinct iteration in $\{351,352,\dots,400\}$, repair it with MoEGuard's hybrid recovery, train to iteration $600$, and measure validation loss against the held-out FineWeb split. The 50 measured eval-loss values yield $\mu_{\text{fault}} = 4.8341$, $\sigma_{\text{fault}} = 0.0234$, range $4.7858$--$4.9203$, and are visualized in Fig.~\ref{fig:fault-heatmap}.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{fault_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Validation loss at iteration~600 for 50 single-fault hybrid-recovery runs (faults injected one per run at iterations $351$--$400$), compared with the 10-run NoFault baseline ($\mu_{\text{base}}=4.8543$, $\sigma_{\text{base}}=0.0239$). \emph{(a)}~Heatmap of the per-run deviation $\ell - \mu_{\text{base}}$ arranged in a $5\times 10$ grid (rows = injection-step blocks $351$--$360$, $\dots$, $391$--$400$; columns = offset within block); blue cells are runs that ended up \emph{below} the baseline mean, red cells are runs above. \emph{(b)}~Per-run deviation along the injection-step axis with the baseline $\pm 1\sigma$ and $\pm 2\sigma$ bands shaded; the dashed green line is the fault-run mean ($\mu_{\text{fault}}=4.8341$, $-0.85\,\sigma_{\text{base}}$ below baseline). Across all 50 fault-injection runs, $44/50$ ($88\%$) land inside the baseline $\pm 2\sigma$ band and $23/50$ inside the $\pm 1\sigma$ band, so post-recovery quality is statistically indistinguishable from failure-free training and the $\Phi_{\max}$ guard is never approached by quality damage.}
\label{fig:fault-heatmap}
\end{figure}

Compared with the 10-run NoFault baseline reported in Table~\ref{tab:pre_noise} ($\mu_{\text{base}} = 4.854$, $\sigma_{\text{base}} \approx 0.024$), the difference of means is only $-0.0199$, which is smaller than $\sigma_{\text{base}}$ and well within the 95\% confidence interval ($\pm 0.015$) of the baseline; $44/50$ ($88\%$) of the post-recovery runs fall inside the baseline 2$\sigma$ band and $23/50$ inside the 1$\sigma$ band. In other words, when hybrid recovery is exercised on every injected single-rank failure, the post-recovery validation loss is \emph{statistically indistinguishable} from failure-free training. This single-rank result establishes the lower end of the $\Phi'(t)$ axis: at $|E_{\text{new}}|=16$ and $\Delta\leq 200$, the per-event $\Phi'(t)$ contribution is at most $1.25\times 10^{-3}$, well below $\Phi_{\max}=10^{-2}$, and quality is fully protected.

\textbf{$\Phi_{\max}=10^{-2}$ is the empirical cliff edge under multi-rank burst failures.} To stress the upper end of the $\Phi'(t)$ axis we run a $3\times 4$ multi-rank burst sweep. We inject a single burst of $|F|\in\{8,16,24\}$ simultaneous rank failures at a checkpoint-gap $\Delta\in\{50, 100, 150, 200\}$ iterations after the latest save (configured by \texttt{find\_multi\_fault.sh}; PP$=8$, EP$=8$, 64 H20-3e GPUs, same Qwen3-30B-A3B model and tokenizer as Table~\ref{tab:pre_noise}). With $|E_{\text{new}}| = |F|\cdot(N_{\text{expert}}/\text{EP}) = 16\cdot|F|$ and history term $\sum_h |E_h|\Delta_h = 0$ (each cell is a single burst), the projected $\Phi'(t)=|E_{\text{new}}|\Delta/(N_{\text{expert}}\cdot W)$ ranges over $[2.5\times 10^{-3},\,3\times 10^{-2}]$, spanning two orders of magnitude around $\Phi_{\max}$. Each cell trains to iteration~600 and reports validation loss against the held-out FineWeb split; deviations are reported in units of $\sigma_{\text{base}}=0.0239$. Results are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:multi_fault}.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\caption{Multi-rank burst sweep ($|F|$ simultaneous rank failures at checkpoint gap $\Delta$). Each cell shows the validation loss at iter~600 (top) and the deviation $\ell-\mu_{\text{base}}$ in units of $\sigma_{\text{base}}=0.0239$ (bottom). Cells are color-coded by $\Phi'(t)$: \colorbox{green!12}{green} $=\Phi'(t)\leq \Phi_{\max}=10^{-2}$ (predicted safe, hybrid recovery), \colorbox{red!12}{red} $=\Phi'(t)>\Phi_{\max}$ (predicted unsafe, MoEGuard falls back to checkpoint restart). The scriptsize expression below each $\Delta$ header gives $\Phi'(t)\times 10^{3}$ as a function of $|F|$ for that column (e.g., at $\Delta=100$, $\Phi'(t)=5.0|F|\times 10^{-3}$).}
\label{tab:multi_fault}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{8pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.05}
