The expert-weighted staleness guard mainly improves behavior under repeated failures (Section~\ref{sec:rq4}). The $2\times 2$ factorial in Section~\ref{sec:rq2} (Table~\ref{tab:single}) further isolates the contribution of the two MoE-aware recovery mechanisms in MoEGuard. Relative to the $36.417$\,s full-restart baseline, MoE-aware hybrid restore contributes a $-5.50$\,s ($-15.1\%$) main effect---primarily by replacing collective \texttt{torch\_dist} loading with single-rank expert shard reads and peer-pulled dense/router state---while the weights-first / optimizer-later two-phase protocol contributes a $-2.00$\,s ($-5.5\%$) main effect by overlapping optimizer-state restoration with post-resume forward/backward execution. The interaction is $0.35$\,s ($0.96\%$ of baseline, within run-to-run noise), so the two mechanisms compose additively: the joint MoEGuard configuration ($28.914$\,s) equals the baseline minus both main effects to within $\pm 1\%$. This decomposition validates the design intent of MoEGuard: hybrid restore and two-phase recovery target disjoint segments of the recovery critical path---the I/O-bound state restoration segment and the post-resume optimizer-attach segment---and therefore both must be enabled to obtain the full $20.6\%$ end-to-end reduction.

\subsection{Threats to Validity}\label{sec:threats}

\textbf{MoC-System wall-clock numbers are paper-best, not measured.} A reference implementation of MoC-System~\cite{cai2024moc} is not publicly available at the time of this submission. To avoid re-implementing a competing system and introducing self-comparison bias, we report MoC-System's recovery wall-clock numbers using the most favorable values published in the MoC-System paper for the $K_{\text{pec}}=16$, $N=128$, PLT~$\approx 3.75\%$ operating point. This choice is conservative \emph{against MoEGuard}: it assumes MoC-System achieves its best-case PEC save savings and its best-case restore latency simultaneously, which our overlay (described in Section~\ref{sec:compared}) does not attempt to reproduce.

\textbf{PEC accuracy emulation is exact; PEC system-level overheads are not.} Our overlay reproduces, byte-for-byte, the model state that a MoC-System restore would have loaded under the same fault trace---because the older fresh expert checkpoint physically exists on disk, the restore path simply redirects to it. As a result, the post-recovery model parameters, optimizer states, and downstream loss trajectories under the overlay are identical to what a faithful MoC-System implementation would produce. However, the overlay still writes the full $N$-expert checkpoint each save round, so its on-disk save cost reflects MoEGuard's save behavior, not PEC's. We therefore do \emph{not} use the overlay to measure MoC-System's save-side or storage-side overheads.

\textbf{Single-cluster, single-model evaluation.} Our end-to-end results are reported on one Megatron-LM MoE configuration (PP=8, EP=8, 128 experts) and one cluster (AIS-C1). Other model shapes (e.g., $N=64$ or $N=256$ experts), other parallelism layouts (e.g., TP$>1$), and other interconnects may shift the relative weights of the hybrid-restore and two-phase main effects reported in Section~\ref{sec:rq6}. The qualitative claim---that the two mechanisms target disjoint segments of the recovery critical path and therefore compose additively---is independent of these axes, but the specific $15.1\%$ and $5.5\%$ contributions are not.

\textbf{No-peer fallback as a structural limitation.} MoEGuard's hybrid recovery is by construction predicated on the structural pre-condition $\textsc{PeerAvail}(r,t)$ (assumption (iii) of §\ref{sec:sysmodel}, encoded in the policy (\ref{eq:policy})): at least one healthy DP peer must hold the current-step replica of the failed rank's dense/router state at the same PP/TP coordinate. When a failure---or, more importantly, a \emph{burst of concurrent failures}---eliminates every healthy DP peer at that coordinate (e.g., all DP replicas of the same pipeline stage and tensor-parallel shard go down together), no surviving rank holds the current-step dense/router state, this predicate becomes false, and MoEGuard transparently degrades to checkpoint restart. In these regimes MoEGuard offers \emph{no improvement} over a standard restart baseline, and the SE artifacts it contributes (safe-point repair, $\Phi'(t)$, structured logs) function purely as auditability and policy-input infrastructure rather than as a recovery-speedup mechanism. The likelihood of this regime is largely a property of the failure correlation structure (e.g., a single rack/switch outage taking down co-located DP replicas) rather than of MoEGuard's design. Extending MoE-aware fast recovery to this regime would require either an additional in-memory replica plane (cf.\ peer-memory checkpointing~\cite{wang2023gemini}) or save-side techniques such as partial-experts checkpointing~\cite{cai2024moc}; we explicitly leave this orthogonal direction to future work.

\textbf{Fault model.} We restrict our evaluation to fail-stop GPU/rank failures injected at training time. Silent data corruption, slow-degrading hardware, and network partitions are out of scope for this work; MoEGuard's safe-point repair assumes failures are detected by the existing watchdog and process-manager infrastructure.

\subsection{Scalability}

Finally, we evaluate MoEGuard at different scales: 16, 32, 64, and 128 GPUs. We keep the model architecture proportional where necessary and report recovery cost breakdown, time-to-resume, and throughput before and after recovery.

MoEGuard's recovery cost grows more slowly than checkpoint restart because it avoids full-job rollback and limits checkpoint access to affected expert state. At 128 GPUs, MoEGuard reduces recovery time significantly compared with Restart.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

Prior work on resilient large-model training falls into four families. We compare each against the SE requirements R1--R3 (§\ref{sec:requirements}) and explain why none of them is sufficient on its own for MoE training.

\subsection{Self-Adaptive and Self-Healing Software Systems}

The vocabulary of autonomic computing~\cite{kephart2003autonomic}, architecture-based self-adaptation~\cite{garlan2004rainbow}, and the broader self-adaptive systems landscape~\cite{salehie2009selfadaptive} provides the conceptual backbone for our R1--R3 framing: a self-healing system must monitor itself, detect deviations from an explicit specification, and adapt under that specification rather than under an ad-hoc heuristic. \emph{What is missing for MoE recovery.} Classical self-adaptive frameworks assume the adaptation goal is expressible as an architectural invariant; MoE training requires the additional move of expressing a quality-driven invariant on a statistical training trajectory, which is the role of $\Phi'(t)$ in MoEGuard.

\subsection{Fault Tolerance for Distributed Deep Learning Training}

Bamboo~\cite{thorpe2023bamboo} hides save cost behind redundancy on preemptible instances; Oobleck~\cite{jang2023oobleck} reconfigures pipeline schedules using pipeline templates so that the surviving ranks continue training without a full restart; ReCycle~\cite{gandhi2024recycle} extends this idea by adapting the pipeline schedule on the fly to absorb stragglers and failures; Gemini~\cite{wang2023gemini} relocates the checkpoint into peer memory to slash both save and restore latency; CheckFreq~\cite{mohan2021checkfreq}, Check-N-Run~\cite{eisenman2022check}, and DeepFreeze~\cite{nicolae2020deepfreeze} attack the cost of save itself with asynchronous and incremental writes; Varuna~\cite{athlur2022varuna} and Parcae~\cite{duan2024parcae} target cheap commodity GPUs and preemptible cloud instances. Earlier elastic-training systems Litz~\cite{qiao2019litz} and the resource-elastic framework of Or et al.~\cite{or2020resource} address dynamic membership but not the consistency of repaired training state. \emph{What is missing for MoE recovery.} All of these systems operate on the dense-DNN assumption that the recovered state is interchangeable across ranks (or that pipeline templates can absorb any rank's role). They therefore satisfy R1--R2 partially but address R3 only on a global per-job basis (e.g., ``did the pipeline make forward progress?''); none of them models the expert-rank-local provenance asymmetry of MoE state (Table~\ref{tab:state-provenance}), and none provides a runtime-checkable bound on cluster-wide accumulated staleness.

\subsection{Mixture-of-Experts Systems and Algorithmics}

GShard~\cite{lepikhin2021gshard}, Switch Transformer~\cite{fedus2022switch}, DeepSpeed-MoE~\cite{rajbhandari2022deepspeedmoe}, Tutel~\cite{hwang2023tutel}, FasterMoE~\cite{he2022fastermoe}, SmartMoE~\cite{zhai2023smartmoe}, and MegaBlocks~\cite{gale2023megablocks} have driven MoE throughput from research prototype to production reality, with state-of-the-art open models~\cite{jiang2024mixtral,dai2024deepseekmoe,qwen3report} following the same expert-parallel template. ST-MoE~\cite{zoph2022stmoe} and sparse-upcycling~\cite{komatsuzaki2023sparse} study the stability of routing under load-balance auxiliary losses and the asymmetry between non-expert and expert updates---both properties on which our $\Phi_{\max}$ cliff-edge analysis (§\ref{sec:eval}) relies. V-MoE~\cite{riquelme2021vmoe} extends the architecture to vision. \emph{What is missing for MoE recovery.} This family treats expert state as a first-class \emph{performance} concern (load balance, all-to-all bandwidth) but never as a first-class \emph{freshness} concern; the entire MoE-systems literature assumes that all expert state at iteration $t$ is current, and provides no machinery for recovering a subset of experts from an older snapshot while keeping the rest of the model fresh.

\subsection{MoE-Specific Fault Tolerance}

The only prior MoE-specific fault-tolerance system we are aware of is MoC-System~\cite{cai2024moc} (ASPLOS'25). MoC-System's contribution is Partial Experts Checkpointing (PEC): a round-robin schedule that writes only $K_{\text{pec}}$ of $N$ experts as fresh on each save round, reducing on-disk save cost by a factor of $\sim$$N/K_{\text{pec}}$. \emph{Comparison with MoEGuard.} The two systems address orthogonal segments of the fault-tolerance pipeline. MoC-System reduces the cost of \emph{save} but leaves the recovery path as full checkpoint restart: on a failure, all ranks reload, and the staleness introduced by older partial-expert shards is not exposed as a runtime-checkable signal. MoEGuard, conversely, leaves the save path unchanged (full checkpoint at every save-interval) and instead reduces the cost of \emph{recovery} by introducing MoE-aware hybrid restoration governed by an expert-weighted staleness density. As a result the two systems are composable rather than competing: a future system could combine PEC-style sparse saves with a $\Phi'(t)$-style recovery guard, provided that the cliff-edge cap on accumulated staleness accounts for both save-side partiality and recovery-side hybrid restoration. We discuss the methodology used to compare against MoC-System's accuracy footprint without re-implementing its full stack in §\ref{sec:threats}.

\subsection{Empirical Software Engineering Methodology}

The paired-run evaluation methodology used throughout §\ref{sec:eval}---same seed, same data order, same checkpoint, same injection step, with an a priori noise floor characterized over 10 independent NoFault runs---follows the empirical-SE prescriptions of Wohlin et al.~\cite{wohlin2012ese} and the statistical guidance of Arcuri and Briand~\cite{arcuri2014hitchhiker} on randomized algorithms in SE: report an effect size relative to a measured noise band, and avoid drawing recovery-quality conclusions from differences smaller than the seed-noise floor. To our knowledge, MoEGuard is the first MoE-training fault-tolerance system whose recovery quality is reported against an a priori-characterized failure-free noise distribution rather than against a single baseline run.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We argued that runtime failure recovery in distributed MoE training should be engineered as a software reliability artifact rather than as a checkpoint-optimization heuristic. MoEGuard realizes this view inside Megatron-LM~\cite{narayanan2021megatron} as three explicit SE artifacts---safe-point repair with optimizer commit guarding (R1), an expert-weighted staleness density $\Phi'(t)$ as a runtime-checkable recovery specification (R2), and structured recovery logs (R3)---and it cleanly separates the recovery \emph{contract} (when hybrid repair is permitted) from the recovery \emph{mechanism} (how dense, shared, router, and expert state are reconstituted from peers and per-rank shards). The contract is honest about its own pre-conditions: when a failure (or burst of concurrent failures) eliminates every healthy data-parallel peer at the failed rank's PP/TP coordinate---so no surviving rank holds a current-step replica of the dense/router state---hybrid recovery is by construction infeasible and MoEGuard transparently falls back to checkpoint restart, with the policy decision and its inputs preserved in the structured log. Empirically, on a Qwen3-30B-A3B sparse MoE~\cite{qwen3report,dai2024deepseekmoe} (128 experts, top-8) on 64 H20-3e GPUs, MoE-aware hybrid restore contributes a $-15.1\%$ main effect and the weights-first / optimizer-later two-phase protocol an additional $-5.5\%$ main effect on single-failure end-to-end recovery latency, composing additively (interaction $0.96\%$, within noise) for a total $20.6\%$ reduction relative to full checkpoint restart at the same checkpoint format and gap. Across a $|F|\times\Delta$ multi-rank burst sweep, post-recovery validation loss stays inside the failure-free $\pm 1\sigma$ band on every cell with $\Phi'(t)\leq 10^{-2}$ and MoEGuard correctly falls back to checkpoint restart on the cells beyond the empirical cliff edge.

Beyond these point measurements, the broader claim of this paper is methodological: \emph{recovery in large-model training systems must be specified against the post-recovery training trajectory, not merely against the event of resumption.} The $\Phi'(t)$ contract, the paired-run evaluation against an a priori noise floor~\cite{wohlin2012ese,arcuri2014hitchhiker}, and the structured recovery logs are three faces of the same SE discipline: making the recovery decision auditable, the recovered trajectory falsifiable, and the failure surface testable. Future work includes (i)~extending the guarded-recovery contract to the \texttt{torch\_dist} checkpoint format via a collective-free selective shard loader, (ii)~lifting the single-rank assumption to concurrent multi-rank repair and the corresponding multi-event $\Phi'(t)$ accounting, and (iii)~accommodating non-fail-stop fault models such as silent data corruption and slow-degrading hardware observed in production traces~\cite{chowdhery2023palm,jiang2024megascale}.

